1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,774
Why hasn't Mehrauli's 6 tons of iron rusted for centuries?

2
00:00:03,774 --> 00:00:08,233
The iron gate of your house turns brown in just a few years.

3
00:00:08,233 --> 00:00:11,664
Even a bicycle chain rusts in the rain.

4
00:00:11,664 --> 00:00:13,722
But in Delhi there is a pillar

5
00:00:13,722 --> 00:00:16,810
that has stood under the open sky for centuries.

6
00:00:16,810 --> 00:00:21,016
Scientists at IIT Kanpur have estimated its weight at about 6 tons.

7
00:00:21,016 --> 00:00:23,820
That is the weight of one big, fully grown elephant in iron.

8
00:00:23,820 --> 00:00:26,624
And this iron was not cast in a mold.

9
00:00:26,624 --> 00:00:28,376
It is called wrought iron,

10
00:00:28,376 --> 00:00:31,180
meaning iron that was heated and beaten with a hammer.

11
00:00:31,180 --> 00:00:35,807
According to ASI measurements, the pillar's total height is 7.21 metres.

12
00:00:35,807 --> 00:00:40,079
A part of it is buried today beneath the stone floor.

13
00:00:40,079 --> 00:00:42,571
At the very top is a bell-shaped head,

14
00:00:42,571 --> 00:00:46,486
called the bell capital. From a distance it looks like a straight, calm,

15
00:00:46,486 --> 00:00:47,910
black pillar.

16
00:00:47,910 --> 00:00:51,089
So the questions are simple. Who had this pillar built,

17
00:00:51,089 --> 00:00:54,267
where did it stand before? And the biggest one:

18
00:00:54,267 --> 00:00:56,651
why hasn't it rusted?

19
00:00:56,651 --> 00:00:59,830
The inscription carved on the pillar is our first witness.

20
00:00:59,830 --> 00:01:04,200
Today we will look at each answer one by one, with proof.

21
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:07,118
Let's first listen to the pillar's own words.

22
00:01:07,118 --> 00:01:10,401
There is an old inscription carved on it in Sanskrit.

23
00:01:10,401 --> 00:01:13,684
An inscription means a message carved on stone or metal.

24
00:01:13,684 --> 00:01:17,332
It is a praise of a king, in the form of a poem.

25
00:01:17,332 --> 00:01:20,250
This is the oldest proof of this story.

26
00:01:20,250 --> 00:01:21,424
The inscription says,

27
00:01:21,424 --> 00:01:25,728
a king named Chandra devoted his heart to Vishnu.

28
00:01:25,728 --> 00:01:28,466
Then this tall flagstaff of Lord Vishnu

29
00:01:28,466 --> 00:01:32,379
was set up on a hill called Vishnupada. Dhwaj means flag,

30
00:01:32,379 --> 00:01:35,900
here it means a sacred pillar in the name of the deity.

31
00:01:35,900 --> 00:01:38,823
Now imagine the scene of that day. A hill,

32
00:01:38,823 --> 00:01:41,382
soft morning sunlight, people gathered below.

33
00:01:41,382 --> 00:01:43,209
Priests are reciting mantras,

34
00:01:43,209 --> 00:01:45,767
the king is standing a little way off, watching.

35
00:01:45,767 --> 00:01:49,421
The shining iron pillar slowly rises upright.

36
00:01:49,421 --> 00:01:52,710
This picture is our imagination, but what matters is the inscription.

37
00:01:52,710 --> 00:01:56,214
The inscription also lists the king's victories. In the land of Vanga,

38
00:01:56,214 --> 00:01:59,368
that is, today's Bengal, enemies came together united.

39
00:01:59,368 --> 00:02:02,522
The king pushed them back with his own chest.

40
00:02:02,522 --> 00:02:05,326
This is the language of poetry, not plain history.

41
00:02:05,326 --> 00:02:08,480
But this tells us about the king's glory.

42
00:02:08,480 --> 00:02:13,800
It further says that in battle the king crossed the seven mouths of the Indus river.

43
00:02:13,800 --> 00:02:16,637
And he defeated the people called the Vahlikas.

44
00:02:16,637 --> 00:02:19,474
The English translation of this inscription was published by the epigraphist J. F.

45
00:02:19,474 --> 00:02:20,893
Fleet.

46
00:02:20,893 --> 00:02:23,730
Even today, most scholars read that same translation.

47
00:02:23,730 --> 00:02:27,014
Now, leaving the inscription aside, let's go by what people say.

48
00:02:27,014 --> 00:02:30,298
In Delhi, a story has been told for generations.

49
00:02:30,298 --> 00:02:33,582
This story is recorded even in old ASI reports.

50
00:02:33,582 --> 00:02:35,881
But please note, this is a folk tale.

51
00:02:35,881 --> 00:02:38,180
There is no proof that it actually happened.

52
00:02:38,180 --> 00:02:41,844
In the story there is Raja Anangpal. A Brahmin tells him,

53
00:02:41,844 --> 00:02:45,142
this pillar is fixed in the head of Vasuki, the king of serpents.

54
00:02:45,142 --> 00:02:48,806
Vasuki, that is, the king of the serpents in the Puranas. The Brahmin said,

55
00:02:48,806 --> 00:02:52,470
as long as the pillar stands, the kingdom will stay in your family.

56
00:02:52,470 --> 00:02:55,071
Doubt arose in the king's mind.

57
00:02:55,071 --> 00:02:58,043
Is the pillar really buried that deep?

58
00:02:58,043 --> 00:03:00,644
He had the pillar pulled out. The story says,

59
00:03:00,644 --> 00:03:03,988
the foot of the pillar came out wet with Vasuki's blood.

60
00:03:03,988 --> 00:03:06,960
The Brahmin's words turned out true, and the king regretted it.

61
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:10,840
The pillar was buried back, but now it stayed loose.

62
00:03:10,840 --> 00:03:14,721
A loose nail, that is a loose peg, was called Dhili Killi.

63
00:03:14,721 --> 00:03:18,989
According to the story, the city's name Dilli came from this.

64
00:03:18,989 --> 00:03:20,542
It's fun to listen to,

65
00:03:20,542 --> 00:03:24,810
but this is a folk explanation of the name, not solid history.

66
00:03:24,810 --> 00:03:26,976
Another tradition was very famous.

67
00:03:26,976 --> 00:03:30,226
People used to stand with their back against the pillar.

68
00:03:30,226 --> 00:03:33,837
Then they would try to join their hands behind them.

69
00:03:33,837 --> 00:03:37,086
It was believed that if the hands met, your luck would open up,

70
00:03:37,086 --> 00:03:40,697
and your wish would be fulfilled. The pillar paid the price for this belief,

71
00:03:40,697 --> 00:03:41,780
we will see that ahead.

72
00:03:41,780 --> 00:03:44,539
Now the biggest question: why did it not rust?

73
00:03:44,539 --> 00:03:46,953
First understand what rust is.

74
00:03:46,953 --> 00:03:50,402
When iron meets the oxygen and moisture in the air,

75
00:03:50,402 --> 00:03:53,506
a brown layer forms. That layer keeps flaking off,

76
00:03:53,506 --> 00:03:55,921
and the iron inside keeps getting eaten away.

77
00:03:55,921 --> 00:03:57,990
This is what we call rust.

78
00:03:57,990 --> 00:04:02,321
On this question, R., a metallurgist at IIT Kanpur, worked for years.

79
00:04:02,321 --> 00:04:04,683
Balasubramaniam. A metallurgist means a metal scientist,

80
00:04:04,683 --> 00:04:07,046
someone who studies the structure of metals.

81
00:04:07,046 --> 00:04:09,802
They took samples from the surface of the pillar.

82
00:04:09,802 --> 00:04:14,920
Then, using a microscope and lab tests, they studied it layer by layer.

83
00:04:14,920 --> 00:04:17,408
In 2002 they presented their findings.

84
00:04:17,408 --> 00:04:20,961
On the surface of the pillar there is a very thin protective layer.

85
00:04:20,961 --> 00:04:22,383
It is called misawite,

86
00:04:22,383 --> 00:04:26,647
a special oxide of iron that is dense and sticks tightly.

87
00:04:26,647 --> 00:04:29,490
This layer doesn't flake off like ordinary rust.

88
00:04:29,490 --> 00:04:33,640
Another substance was found in this layer: iron hydrogen phosphate hydrate.

89
00:04:33,640 --> 00:04:35,903
The name is heavy, but the meaning is simple.

90
00:04:35,903 --> 00:04:40,053
It is a crystal-like material made of phosphorus and iron.

91
00:04:40,053 --> 00:04:43,070
Misawite and this phosphate together form a shield.

92
00:04:43,070 --> 00:04:47,220
This shield stops air and water from getting inside.

93
00:04:47,220 --> 00:04:49,146
So the pillar was not saved from rust,

94
00:04:49,146 --> 00:04:51,715
it was saved by a special kind of rust.

95
00:04:51,715 --> 00:04:53,641
The thin layer that formed at the start

96
00:04:53,641 --> 00:04:56,852
slowly turned into a shield. This is no miracle,

97
00:04:56,852 --> 00:04:58,778
it's plain science. And honestly,

98
00:04:58,778 --> 00:05:02,310
this is what makes this pillar even more amazing.

99
00:05:02,310 --> 00:05:05,078
Now let's look at the root of this shield.

100
00:05:05,078 --> 00:05:10,615
In IIT Kanpur's tests, the iron of the pillar had an average of 0.25 percent phosphorus.

101
00:05:10,615 --> 00:05:12,197
Phosphorus is an element

102
00:05:12,197 --> 00:05:16,152
that is usually considered an impurity in iron.

103
00:05:16,152 --> 00:05:18,920
But here, this very impurity became the hero.

104
00:05:18,920 --> 00:05:20,065
Now compare.

105
00:05:20,065 --> 00:05:25,788
In today's wrought iron, phosphorus rarely goes above 0.05 percent.

106
00:05:25,788 --> 00:05:29,222
So this pillar has many times more phosphorus.

107
00:05:29,222 --> 00:05:33,419
This extra phosphorus helps the protective layer to form.

108
00:05:33,419 --> 00:05:36,090
The first lock of the secret opens right here.

109
00:05:36,090 --> 00:05:38,303
So where did all this phosphorus come from?

110
00:05:38,303 --> 00:05:41,622
The answer is hidden in the furnaces of the ancient Indian craftsmen.

111
00:05:41,622 --> 00:05:44,204
They made iron in a bloomery furnace.

112
00:05:44,204 --> 00:05:46,048
A bloomery is a small, open furnace,

113
00:05:46,048 --> 00:05:49,367
in which raw iron ore was heated with coal.

114
00:05:49,367 --> 00:05:51,580
This gave iron directly.

115
00:05:51,580 --> 00:05:55,089
In today's factories, lime is added to the furnace,

116
00:05:55,089 --> 00:05:56,492
it is called flux.

117
00:05:56,492 --> 00:06:00,351
Flux is something that pulls out impurities and carries them away.

118
00:06:00,351 --> 00:06:03,158
Lime carries away the phosphorus too.

119
00:06:03,158 --> 00:06:05,263
The ancient craftsmen didn't add lime.

120
00:06:05,263 --> 00:06:08,070
So the phosphorus stayed inside the iron.

121
00:06:08,070 --> 00:06:12,898
Then the pieces of hot iron were joined by hammering them again and again.

122
00:06:12,898 --> 00:06:14,755
This is called forge welding,

123
00:06:14,755 --> 00:06:18,469
that is, joining metal by heating it and hammering it. Just think how many craftsmen,

124
00:06:18,469 --> 00:06:20,697
how many furnaces, how many days it must have taken.

125
00:06:20,697 --> 00:06:24,040
The names of those craftsmen are nowhere in the inscription.

126
00:06:24,040 --> 00:06:28,620
Now let us move to the part where scholars do not agree.

127
00:06:28,620 --> 00:06:32,054
First question: who exactly was Raja Chandra of the inscription?

128
00:06:32,054 --> 00:06:34,344
The inscription has only the name, Chandra.

129
00:06:34,344 --> 00:06:37,779
Neither the name of the dynasty is clear, nor any year.

130
00:06:37,779 --> 00:06:40,450
That is why this puzzle remains unsolved to this day.

131
00:06:40,450 --> 00:06:44,276
One side says he was the Gupta emperor Chandragupta II.

132
00:06:44,276 --> 00:06:47,719
Dwitiya means second, the second Gupta king with this name.

133
00:06:47,719 --> 00:06:51,162
The Gupta dynasty was a great royal dynasty of ancient India.

134
00:06:51,162 --> 00:06:54,222
The name Chandra matches Chandragupta,

135
00:06:54,222 --> 00:06:56,900
this is the straightforward argument of this side.

136
00:06:56,900 --> 00:06:58,315
The other side says,

137
00:06:58,315 --> 00:07:01,852
he could be Raja Chandravarman of Pushkarana in Rajasthan.

138
00:07:01,852 --> 00:07:07,158
In studies linked to the ASI, this question about Chandra's identity was raised seriously.

139
00:07:07,158 --> 00:07:08,927
Both sides have their own arguments.

140
00:07:08,927 --> 00:07:12,110
And no final decision has been reached yet.

141
00:07:12,110 --> 00:07:15,790
So what do we have in hand? The name is certain, Chandra.

142
00:07:15,790 --> 00:07:17,999
The devotion is also certain, to Vishnu.

143
00:07:17,999 --> 00:07:20,943
But the king's face is still hazy.

144
00:07:20,943 --> 00:07:23,151
This often happens in history,

145
00:07:23,151 --> 00:07:25,728
when a single inscription is the only witness.

146
00:07:25,728 --> 00:07:29,040
And a witness says only as much as is written.

147
00:07:29,040 --> 00:07:33,122
Second puzzle: where was the pillar earlier? The inscription says,

148
00:07:33,122 --> 00:07:37,205
on the Vishnupada hill. But today it is in Delhi.

149
00:07:37,205 --> 00:07:42,194
The scholar who reads inscriptions is called an epigraphist. Epigraphist J. F.

150
00:07:42,194 --> 00:07:46,730
Fleet said in 1898 that this hill was in Mathura.

151
00:07:46,730 --> 00:07:48,764
Then R. from IIT Kanpur,

152
00:07:48,764 --> 00:07:52,424
Balasubramaniam, and researcher Meera Dass gave a different opinion.

153
00:07:52,424 --> 00:07:56,899
According to them, Vishnupada is today's Udayagiri, near Vidisha.

154
00:07:56,899 --> 00:08:00,559
They put forward evidence from both archaeology and the sky.

155
00:08:00,559 --> 00:08:04,220
Archaeology means the study of old things found from the ground.

156
00:08:04,220 --> 00:08:06,190
Their argument is linked to the sun.

157
00:08:06,190 --> 00:08:08,818
Summer solstice means the longest day of the year.

158
00:08:08,818 --> 00:08:12,759
They connected the line of the sun on that day with the location of the hill.

159
00:08:12,759 --> 00:08:15,714
This work of Meera Dass came out in 2004.

160
00:08:15,714 --> 00:08:18,670
Mathura or Udayagiri, this debate continues even today.

161
00:08:18,670 --> 00:08:21,872
The third piece of evidence is also buried in the pillar itself.

162
00:08:21,872 --> 00:08:26,274
A short inscription, dated Samvat 1109. Samvat means Vikram Samvat,

163
00:08:26,274 --> 00:08:28,275
an old Indian calendar.

164
00:08:28,275 --> 00:08:33,478
In today's English calendar, this falls around 1052 CE.

165
00:08:33,478 --> 00:08:36,680
CE, the one we write every day.

166
00:08:36,680 --> 00:08:41,050
The archaeologist Alexander Cunningham read this inscription. An archaeologist is someone

167
00:08:41,050 --> 00:08:43,832
who studies excavations and old evidence.

168
00:08:43,832 --> 00:08:48,600
According to him, it says that the Tomar king Anangpal founded Delhi.

169
00:08:48,600 --> 00:08:52,970
So this inscription talks about the founding of a city.

170
00:08:52,970 --> 00:08:54,947
Later, the archaeologist B. R.

171
00:08:54,947 --> 00:08:57,716
Mani read the same inscription differently.

172
00:08:57,716 --> 00:09:00,880
According to him, Anangpal fixed the pillar firmly,

173
00:09:00,880 --> 00:09:05,230
that is, he planted it again, strongly. In one reading a city is founded,

174
00:09:05,230 --> 00:09:08,394
in the other the pillar is set in place. And remember,

175
00:09:08,394 --> 00:09:13,140
in the Vasuki folk tale too, it was the same king and the same pillar.

176
00:09:13,140 --> 00:09:16,434
Now here is what nobody knows for sure.

177
00:09:16,434 --> 00:09:19,361
If the pillar was earlier in Mathura or Udayagiri,

178
00:09:19,361 --> 00:09:23,387
how did it come to Delhi? Nearly 6 tons of iron, no truck,

179
00:09:23,387 --> 00:09:26,314
no crane. How it was carried so far,

180
00:09:26,314 --> 00:09:28,510
there is no written record of it.

181
00:09:28,510 --> 00:09:30,357
Was it pulled by elephants?

182
00:09:30,357 --> 00:09:33,313
Was it brought by boat along a river?

183
00:09:33,313 --> 00:09:35,900
Was it rolled along on wooden logs?

184
00:09:35,900 --> 00:09:38,486
These are all just guesses, not proof.

185
00:09:38,486 --> 00:09:42,920
Maybe one day an old document or an excavation will give the answer.

186
00:09:42,920 --> 00:09:46,683
There is one more question, and it is tied right to the ground.

187
00:09:46,683 --> 00:09:50,103
1.12 metres of the pillar is under the floor today.

188
00:09:50,103 --> 00:09:53,181
What is down there cannot be seen with the naked eye.

189
00:09:53,181 --> 00:09:55,918
How such a heavy pillar was stood up straight

190
00:09:55,918 --> 00:09:57,970
still amazes people today.

191
00:09:57,970 --> 00:09:59,119
Now it's your turn.

192
00:09:59,119 --> 00:10:03,716
Today you can go to Mehrauli and see this pillar with your own eyes.

193
00:10:03,716 --> 00:10:06,781
But you can no longer hug it.

194
00:10:06,781 --> 00:10:11,761
In 1997 the ASI put an iron railing around the pillar.

195
00:10:11,761 --> 00:10:14,060
A railing means a fence, a protective barrier.

196
00:10:14,060 --> 00:10:15,837
The reason was that same old tradition.

197
00:10:15,837 --> 00:10:20,811
The rubbing of countless hands and sweat was wearing away the thin layer of misawite underneath.

198
00:10:20,811 --> 00:10:22,943
The shield that had formed over centuries

199
00:10:22,943 --> 00:10:25,785
was being worn away by people's love.

200
00:10:25,785 --> 00:10:28,983
That is why the ASI made a little distance in between.

201
00:10:28,983 --> 00:10:31,470
Now wishes are made from a distance.

202
00:10:31,470 --> 00:10:32,482
When you go,

203
00:10:32,482 --> 00:10:35,856
look carefully at the lower part of the pillar.

204
00:10:35,856 --> 00:10:39,566
Look at the bell-shaped capital on top, and look for the lines of the inscription.

205
00:10:39,566 --> 00:10:42,265
You will be standing in front of a piece of evidence

206
00:10:42,265 --> 00:10:44,627
that belongs to both science and history.

207
00:10:44,627 --> 00:10:48,000
And it still stands today under the open sky.

208
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:51,892
In the next episode we will go to Udayagiri, near Vidisha. That same hill,

209
00:10:51,892 --> 00:10:54,368
which some scholars believe is the real Vishnupada.

210
00:10:54,368 --> 00:10:57,906
We will see what the relationship is between the sun and the stone there.

211
00:10:57,906 --> 00:11:01,090
And whether any trace of the pillar is left there.

212
00:11:01,090 --> 00:11:02,828
Now it's your turn.

213
00:11:02,828 --> 00:11:08,041
According to you, where did this pillar stand earlier,

214
00:11:08,041 --> 00:11:13,253
Mathura or Udayagiri? Write just one word in the comments.

215
00:11:13,253 --> 00:11:19,625
And if you ever hugged it with your back to it,

216
00:11:19,625 --> 00:11:23,100
then please share that story too.
